{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- live_context_annotate: Create, resolve, or list durable annotations for exact context sources\n- live_context_recall: Recall a bounded exact source from a durable live-context annotation\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Use live_context_annotate before compacting an exact source that future work must preserve; choose pin only when its exact text must remain visible, continuity for a visible obligation and recall pointer, or archive for recall-only provenance.\n- Use live_context_recall only when an active continuity pointer or archived source is needed; request the smallest useful maxTokens bound.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-16-finder-stage/AGENTS.md\">\n## Agent skills\n\n### Issue tracker\n\nIssues are tracked as local markdown files under `.scratch/`. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.\n\n### Triage labels\n\nDefault label vocabulary. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.\n\n### Domain docs\n\nSingle-context layout. See `docs/agents/domain.md`.\n\n## Layout\n\nTwo implementations live side by side until the M6 cutover\n(`.scratch/ts-rewrite/`, ADR-0004): the Go CLI (`cmd/roast`,\n`internal/`) and its TypeScript rewrite (`src/`, `test/`, `scripts/`).\nBoth are under active change. `npm run test:go` runs the Go and launcher\ntests; `npm test` runs typecheck and the TS suite; lint is\n`npm run lint`.\n\n## Area notes\n\nBefore editing code in an area, read its notes. Each area file holds the\nGo and TS notes for that area side by side.\n\n- Any Go code or Go test: `docs/agents/go.md`\n- Any TS in `src/`, `test/`, or `scripts/`: `docs/agents/typescript.md`\n- Config loading, roles, providers, the `init` starter,\n  `roast.schema.json` (`internal/config`, `src/config/`):\n  `docs/agents/config.md`\n- Trust and approvals (`internal/trust`, `src/trust/`, `*/trust.*`):\n  `docs/agents/trust.md`\n- Stored credentials, `login`, `logout` (`internal/credentials`,\n  `src/credentials/`, `internal/cli/*login*`): `docs/agents/auth.md`\n- Gate and the pre-commit hook (`internal/gate`, `src/gate/`):\n  `docs/agents/gate.md`\n- Agent skill install (`internal/skill`, `src/skill/`):\n  `docs/agents/skill.md`\n- Run records (`internal/record`): `docs/agents/records.md`\n- Scope, context, exploration tools, snapshot denylist (`src/scope/`,\n  `src/context/`, `src/snapshot/`): `docs/agents/scope-context.md`\n- LLM adapters and the agent loop (`src/llm/`): `docs/agents/llm.md`\n- npm packaging, the launcher, release (`packages/`, `scripts/build.ts`,\n  `src/launcher.ts`): `docs/agents/packaging.md`\n\nA new note goes in its area's file; a new area gets a file and a line\nhere. This file holds only what most tasks hit.\n\n## Traps every task hits\n\n- `JSON.parse` syntax errors can quote the input. Credential-file parse\n  errors must use value-free messages so diagnostic output cannot expose\n  a stored key.\n- Shell hooks block Bash commands that contain a heredoc mentioning\n  `process.env.PATH`, a heredoc or command containing `env:` or `.env`\n  text, or a `credentials*` path together with `cat`, `sed`, `head`, and\n  the like. Read and write such files with the editor tools.\n- Tests that write project state (run records, trust, stored\n  credentials) chdir into a temporary Git repository first. `.roast/`\n  ignores itself, so a leak into this checkout is invisible to\n  `git status`; check with `ls -a`.\n- CLI diagnostic tests that resolve roles need valid test credentials\n  and local fake-server routing, even when asserting only local checks.\n- `.npmrc` sets `force=true` so npm links workspaces whose `os`/`cpu`\n  differ from the host. Expect \"using --force\" warnings from every npm\n  command.\n- The CLI parser preserves dashed option names and disables boolean\n  negation. Declare flags such as `no-browser` explicitly and read\n  `args['no-browser']`, rather than a camel-case property.\n- `simulateTerminals` only bypasses interactive-terminal detection. To\n  exercise raw-mode prompt input through the CLI, give stdin an `isTTY`\n  value of `true` and a `setRawMode` function; assert raw mode is restored.\n\n## Docs\n\n- User-facing docs (`README.md`, `docs/`, the bundled\n  `internal/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md`) write every invocation as\n  `npx roast ...`; Roast is a project dependency, never a `PATH` binary.\n  Settings are documented as `.roast.json` keys, never environment\n  variables; only credentials, `ROAST_SKIP`, and test-only `ROAST_LIVE_*`\n  / `ROAST_TEST_*` variables exist.\n- CLI messages in `internal/cli` (for example the gate hint printed\n  during review) still say bare `roast`; changing them is a code change,\n  not a docs change.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nResolve relative paths against the skill's directory (parent of SKILL.md). Use absolute paths in tool calls.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tiiny-routing</name>\n    <description>Delegate subtasks to the local Tiiny device, each on the model that fits its shape. Use when a batch mixes research, indexing, coding, browser and drafting work and should run on the Tiiny appliance instead of a paid model.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-16-finder-stage/.pi/skills/tiiny-routing/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>amazon-bedrock</name>\n    <description>Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up Knowledge Bases, creating agents, applying guardrails, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations (4.5 to 4.6 to 4.7), chunking strategies, API selection (Converse vs InvokeModel), guardrail capabilities, and model selection. Also covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/amazon-bedrock/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-ai-ml</name>\n    <description>Selects, deploys, and customizes AI models on Amazon SageMaker. Fine-tuning (SFT, DPO, RLVR, RLAIF), model selection, dataset preparation, evaluation, deployment to SageMaker endpoints or Bedrock, and endpoint diagnostics. Covers the full lifecycle from planning through production. Use when fine-tuning models on SageMaker, choosing/selecting which base model to customize or fine-tune from SageMaker Hub, finding a model to deploy without fine-tuning, transforming datasets for training, checking data readiness, evaluating model quality, deploying to endpoints, setting up IAM roles and S3 buckets for training jobs, or managing a SageMaker Managed MLflow app. Also use to check endpoint health, diagnose failures, debug latency or errors, or view container logs and CloudWatch metrics. Covers Serverless Model Customization, Nova and OSS deployment paths, and PySDK v3 usage. NOT for Ground Truth labeling, Feature Store, or general-purpose AWS infrastructure.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-ai-ml/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-auth</name>\n    <description>Adds user authentication to web and mobile apps with Amazon Cognito (user pools and identity pools) and the AWS Amplify client auth libraries. Covers sign-up/sign-in flows and the login page (Cognito-hosted UI / managed login), MFA, password policies, OAuth 2.0 / OIDC flows (auth-code + PKCE, client credentials), social/SAML federation, tokens (ID/access/refresh, rotation, revocation, storage), Cognito Lambda triggers, identity pools (temp AWS creds), and gating API Gateway (or ALB) routes to signed-in users via Cognito/JWT authorizers. Applies when adding a login or sign-up page, configuring a user pool or app client, choosing user pool vs identity pool, wiring social/SAML, refreshing tokens, requiring sign-in on an API Gateway or ALB, or debugging redirect_uri/token/MFA/CORS/federation errors. Does NOT cover Amplify Gen2 backend definitions (defineAuth, npx ampx → aws-amplify), IAM/STS/Identity Center (→ aws-iam), or API Gateway/Lambda resource config beyond the authorizer (→ aws-serverless).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-auth/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-billing-and-cost-management</name>\n    <description>Analyze AWS costs, find savings, manage budgets, evaluate Savings Plans and Reserved Instances, right-size EC2/Lambda/RDS/EBS with Compute Optimizer, look up service pricing, query CUR with Athena, detect cost anomalies, scope costs to billing views, and monitor Free Tier usage. Triggers on: AWS bill, cost analysis, reduce spend, savings plan, reserved instance, right-size, budget alert, cost optimization, pricing, free tier, cost anomaly, CUR, cost audit, billing view, billing view ARN.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-billing-and-cost-management/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-blocks</name>\n    <description>Guides building full-stack applications with AWS Blocks — an Infrastructure-from-Code framework. Applies when creating APIs, selecting Building Blocks (KVStore, DistributedTable, Database, AuthBasic, AuthCognito, Realtime, AsyncJob, FileBucket, etc.), running local development, or deploying AWS Blocks applications. Also covers AWS Blocks topics with validated, version-specific patterns that prevent common mistakes. Triggers when user mentions AWS Blocks; project has aws-blocks/ directory; code imports @aws-blocks packages.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-blocks/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-cdk</name>\n    <description>Authors, deploys, and troubleshoots AWS infrastructure using CDK with TypeScript or Python. Covers best practices, stack architecture, and construct patterns. Applies when writing CDK constructs, bootstrapping environments, running cdk deploy/synth/diff, fixing CDK or CloudFormation errors, planning stack structure, importing existing resources, resolving drift, or refactoring stacks without resource replacement.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-cdk/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-cloudformation</name>\n    <description>Authors, validates, and troubleshoots AWS CloudFormation templates. Covers template authoring with secure defaults, pre-deployment validation (cfn-lint, cfn-guard, change sets), CloudFormation Express mode for faster deployments, and root-cause diagnosis of failed stacks using CloudFormation events and CloudTrail correlation.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-cloudformation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-compute</name>\n    <description>Provisions, scales, and operates Amazon EC2 virtual-machine workloads: instance-type selection (Graviton/Arm64, burstable T credits, GPU, instance store vs EBS), launch templates, Auto Scaling groups (scaling policies, instance refresh, mixed instances, Spot, warm pools, lifecycle hooks), IMDSv2, placement groups, Elastic IPs, AMI lifecycle, and Systems Manager fleet operations (Session Manager, Run Command, Patch Manager). Applies to EC2 instance and fleet questions, InsufficientInstanceCapacity, CPU-credit/surplus charges, IMDSv2 401s, instances stuck in Pending:Wait, ASG not replacing unhealthy instances, status-check failures, SSH refused/timed out, or instances missing as SSM managed nodes. For a single secure instance launch, the launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices skill is more appropriate; for instance profiles, see setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles; for Image Builder, see amazon-ec2-image-builder. Does NOT cover Lambda, ECS/Fargate, EKS, VPC/ALB/NLB design, or IAM policy authoring.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-compute/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-containers</name>\n    <description>Builds and deploys containerized workloads on Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS), Elastic Container Service (ECS), Fargate, and ECR (Elastic Container Registry). Covers general EKS knowledge, Karpenter, AWS Load Balancer Controller and leveraging various open source Kubernetes projects with EKS. Covers general ECS knowledge, task definitions, Fargate services, ECS Exec, ECS Express Mode and ECS Managed Instances. Covers general Elastic Beanstalk knowledge, Elastic Beanstalk configuration and platforms supported by Elastic Beanstalk. Covers general ECR knowledge, ECR repository setup and lifecycle policies. Applies when deploying, debugging, or optimizing containers on AWS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-containers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-database</name>\n    <description>Routes any task involving AWS databases — choosing, comparing, recommending, getting started with, or operating a database — to the correct service-specific skill. Supersedes general training-data knowledge with post-training service updates, corrected limitations, and decision procedures for relational (Aurora, DSQL, RDS), key-value (DynamoDB), wide-column (Keyspaces), document (DocumentDB), graph (Neptune), time-series (Timestream), and in-memory/caching (ElastiCache, MemoryDB) workloads. Activates when a user describes building an application on AWS that will store, retrieve, or manage data, even if they do not mention &apos;database&apos; explicitly.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-database/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-deployment</name>\n    <description>Configures CI/CD pipelines using AWS CodePipeline, CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, CodeConnections, and CodeArtifact. Covers CodePipeline V2 (triggers, variables, execution modes, cross-account), buildspec.yml (caching, VPC, Docker), CodeDeploy strategies (blue/green, canary, linear), CodeArtifact (private package registries, auth tokens, cross-account), and source connections (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket). Applies when CodePipeline, CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, CodeConnections, CodeArtifact, buildspec.yml, appspec.yml, or CI/CD pipeline orchestration is referenced. Does NOT cover: ECS Fargate services or task definitions (use aws-containers), CDK Pipelines or cdk deploy (use aws-cdk), sam deploy (use aws-serverless), Amplify deployments (use aws-amplify), or GitHub Actions/GitLab CI.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-deployment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-iam</name>\n    <description>Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-iam/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-messaging-and-streaming</name>\n    <description>Guides general use of AWS messaging and streaming services. Covers Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge, Amazon MQ, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, Amazon Data Firehose, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK).  Use when reasoning about messaging and streaming patterns. Also identifies which AWS service owns each customer communication channel: email (Amazon SES), and WhatsApp, SMS, MMS, RCS, voice and mobile push (the AWS End User Messaging family of services). Routes the request to the specialized skill for that channel. Defers to the channel&apos;s specialized skill when the user already named a specific channel. In general, use specific skills or documentation searches for detailed service-specific questions. Do NOT use for MSK or Managed Service for Apache Flink questions, prefer specific skills. Does not configure customer communication channels; defers to specific skills.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-messaging-and-streaming/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-networking</name>\n    <description>Routes AWS networking requests to the correct service skill for implementation. Covers Route 53 (DNS, health checks, routing policies, Resolver, DNS Firewall), CloudFront (caching, edge, OAC, mTLS, signed URLs), Transit Gateway (multi-VPC hub, segmentation, centralized egress), Direct Connect (hybrid link, DX Gateway, MACsec), Site-to-Site VPN (IPsec tunnels, static or BGP), WAF (web ACLs, AWS Managed Rules, rate-based rules, Bot and Fraud Control), and Shield Advanced (L3/L4 DDoS). Applicable when creating, configuring, troubleshooting, or designing across these services, choosing between them, or diagnosing connectivity or traffic-filtering issues. Not for VPC subnets and route tables, load balancers, VPC endpoints, PrivateLink, API Gateway, IAM policy logic, container or serverless networking, or IaC authoring.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-networking/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-observability</name>\n    <description>Builds, configures, debugs, and optimizes AWS observability with CloudWatch (Log Insights, Metrics, Alarms, Dashboards, EMF), X-Ray, CloudTrail, and ADOT (AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry), AND enables/onboards services to Application Signals using ADOT auto-instrumentation SDKs. Covers Log Insights queries, alarms (metric, composite, anomaly), dashboards, custom metrics/EMF, X-Ray tracing and sampling, ADOT collector config, CloudTrail auditing, and end-to-end Application Signals enablement via ADOT SDKs (CloudWatch Observability EKS add-on, CloudWatch Agent IAM, OTLP endpoints, ServiceEvents, Dynamic Instrumentation), breakpoint and snapshot in Dynamic Instrumentation, live data capture in running service, debug without redeploying. Applies to CloudWatch, alarms, dashboards, EMF, X-Ray, traces, CloudTrail,  ADOT, monitoring, synthetics/canaries, OR enabling/onboarding/instrumenting a service for Application Signals. Not for app logging or security threat detection.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-observability/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-sdk-js-v3-usage</name>\n    <description>AWS SDK for JavaScript v3 development patterns. Use when writing JavaScript or TypeScript code that uses AWS services via @aws-sdk/* packages (aws-sdk-js-v3), or when asked about schemas, runtime validation, serialization, or code generation in the context of the JS/TS AWS SDK.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-sdk-js-v3-usage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-sdk-python-usage</name>\n    <description>AWS SDK for Python (boto3/botocore) development patterns. You MUST use this skill when writing Python code that uses AWS services via boto3 or botocore. This includes creating service clients or resources, configuring sessions and credentials, handling errors with ClientError, using paginators and waiters, S3 file transfers and presigned URLs, DynamoDB table operations, and any boto3/botocore client configuration. Use this skill whenever Python code imports boto3 or botocore, or when the user asks about AWS operations in Python.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-sdk-python-usage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-sdk-swift-usage</name>\n    <description>AWS SDK for Swift development patterns. Use when writing Swift code that uses AWS services via aws-sdk-swift package.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-sdk-swift-usage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-security</name>\n    <description>Covers AWS security services and workflows — Security Hub V2 (OCSF) findings, connectors, aggregators, automation rules, and security posture summaries; Security Hub CSPM (V1/ASFF) controls and compliance standards; GuardDuty threat findings; Inspector vulnerability findings; Macie sensitive data findings; Detective investigation; and Security Lake configuration and data aggregation. Applicable when questions involve security posture, Exposure findings, CSPM failed controls, threat findings, vulnerability findings, sensitive data findings, automation rules, or cross-service security configuration across AWS environments. Procedures use standard AWS CLI syntax and work with or without the AWS MCP server.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-security/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-serverless</name>\n    <description>Builds, deploys, manages, debugs, configures, and optimizes serverless applications on AWS using Lambda, API Gateway, Step Functions, EventBridge, and SAM/CDK. Covers cold starts, CORS debugging, event source mappings, troubleshooting, concurrency, SnapStart, Powertools, function URLs, EventBridge Scheduler, Lambda layers, and production readiness. Triggers on mentions of Lambda, API Gateway, Step Functions, SAM templates, CDK serverless stacks, DynamoDB stream triggers, SQS event sources, cold starts, timeouts, 502/504 errors, throttling, concurrency, CORS, Powertools, or any event-driven architecture on AWS, even without the word &quot;serverless.&quot; Does not apply to EC2, ECS/Fargate containers, or Amplify hosting.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-serverless/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-storage</name>\n    <description>Selects, investigates, and compares AWS object, file, and block storage services, and answers cost, performance, configuration, security, and troubleshooting questions about storage services. Applies when a user asks where to store or archive data based on their usage patterns; which storage service to choose or how two compare; how to migrate data from on-premises or between AWS services; how to protect, replicate, or recover data; how to optimize storage costs; where to deploy shared NFS, SMB, or POSIX file systems; where to store vector embeddings or tabular data; what storage backs enterprise file shares, self-managed databases on EC2, VMware, or stateful containers; or asks what an AWS storage service can do or how it works. Relevant for storage needs for workloads such as AI/ML, analytics, EDA, HPC, media, genomics, or financial trading. Not applicable for SQL query engines (Athena, Spark, Redshift, EMR), ETL (Glue), streaming (Kafka, MSK, Kinesis), or managed database services (RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-storage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>launch-with-aws</name>\n    <description>Migrates vibe-coded web applications to AWS. Handles the full workflow from analysis through migration to deployment, producing deployable AWS Blocks infrastructure code. Supports full-stack apps built with vibe-coding platforms (Lovable, Bolt.new, Replit) and frontend web applications and websites: React, Vue, Angular, Next.js, Nuxt, Astro, SvelteKit, Gatsby, Vite, Svelte, Solid, Docusaurus, and others (static sites, SPAs, and SSR frameworks with static export). Triggers on: launch with AWS, launch on AWS, deploy to AWS, migrate to AWS, host my app on AWS, move my app to AWS, transfer my app to AWS. Activates when the user wants to migrate a vibe-coded app or frontend web app to AWS, even if they don&apos;t say &apos;migrate&apos; explicitly.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/launch-with-aws/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>signing-in-to-aws</name>\n    <description>Gets AWS credentials for CLI/SDK access via `aws login`. Activates when a developer needs to authenticate to AWS for local development, when an AWS operation fails due to missing or expired credentials, or when someone asks about setting up AWS access. Triggers: &quot;set up AWS&quot;, &quot;configure AWS&quot;, &quot;aws login&quot;, &quot;get credentials&quot;, &quot;authenticate&quot;, &quot;session expired&quot;, &quot;token expired&quot;, &quot;no credentials&quot;, &quot;AccessDeniedException&quot; with no configured credentials.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/signing-in-to-aws/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>a11y</name>\n    <description>Run a project-wide WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility audit of a web app: axe-core scans of every discoverable page in the running dev server, static eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y analysis of JSX/TSX source, or both, then a prioritized violation report with fixes. Use when the user asks for an accessibility audit, a11y scan, or WCAG compliance check. For debugging one element&apos;s ARIA, focus, or keyboard behavior in a live page, use chrome-devtools-mcp:a11y-debugging instead.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/a11y/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>adapt</name>\n    <description>Adapt designs to work across different screen sizes, devices, contexts, or platforms. Implements breakpoints, fluid layouts, and touch targets. Use when the user mentions responsive design, mobile layouts, breakpoints, viewport adaptation, or cross-device compatibility.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/adapt/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>adversarial-review</name>\n    <description>Adversarial review of two git worktrees to find bugs/quality issues before merge. Do not use for simple diffs or neutral walkthroughs.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/adversarial-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>animate</name>\n    <description>Review a feature and enhance it with purposeful animations, micro-interactions, and motion effects that improve usability and delight. Use when the user mentions adding animation, transitions, micro-interactions, motion design, hover effects, or making the UI feel more alive.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/animate/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>architecture-inventory</name>\n    <description>Maintain `docs/architecture/` when the user asks to update the runtime architecture inventory or a workflow explicitly requires that inventory. Use report-only mode for an explicit audit or review request.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/architecture-inventory/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>audit</name>\n    <description>Audit a web project for accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, and common frontend defects when the user asks for a technical quality review.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/audit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>bolder</name>\n    <description>Amplify safe or boring designs to make them more visually interesting and stimulating. Increases impact while maintaining usability. Use when the user says the design looks bland, generic, too safe, lacks personality, or wants more visual impact and character.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/bolder/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>clarify</name>\n    <description>Improve unclear UX copy, error messages, microcopy, labels, and instructions to make interfaces easier to understand. Use when the user mentions confusing text, unclear labels, bad error messages, hard-to-follow instructions, or wanting better UX writing.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/clarify/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>colorize</name>\n    <description>Add strategic color to features that are too monochromatic or lack visual interest, making interfaces more engaging and expressive. Use when the user mentions the design looking gray, dull, lacking warmth, needing more color, or wanting a more vibrant or expressive palette.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/colorize/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>critique</name>\n    <description>Review a frontend design or user experience when the user asks for critique, evaluation, or actionable design feedback.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/critique/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>delight</name>\n    <description>Add moments of joy, personality, and unexpected touches that make interfaces memorable and enjoyable to use. Elevates functional to delightful. Use when the user asks to add polish, personality, animations, micro-interactions, delight, or make an interface feel fun or memorable.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/delight/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>distill</name>\n    <description>Strip designs to their essence by removing unnecessary complexity. Great design is simple, powerful, and clean. Use when the user asks to simplify, declutter, reduce noise, remove elements, or make a UI cleaner and more focused.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/distill/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>openai-docs</name>\n    <description>Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or model upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize OpenAI docs MCP tools, use bundled references only as helper context, and restrict any fallback browsing to official OpenAI domains.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/docs/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>find-skills</name>\n    <description>Find and install an agent skill when the user explicitly asks for a skill, an installable workflow, or help extending the agent&apos;s capabilities.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/find-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>git-guardrails-claude-code</name>\n    <description>Set up Claude Code hooks to block dangerous git commands (push, reset --hard, clean, branch -D, etc.) before they execute. Use when user wants to prevent destructive git operations, add git safety hooks, or block git push/reset in Claude Code.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/git-guardrails/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>grilling</name>\n    <description>Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any &apos;grill&apos; trigger phrases.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>layout</name>\n    <description>Improve layout, spacing, and visual rhythm. Fixes monotonous grids, inconsistent spacing, and weak visual hierarchy. Use when the user mentions layout feeling off, spacing issues, visual hierarchy, crowded UI, alignment problems, or wanting better composition.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/layout/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>modern-web-guidance</name>\n    <description>Search tool for modern web development best practices. MANDATORY: Execute FIRST for all HTML/CSS and clientside JS tasks. Do NOT skip — web APIs evolve rapidly and training weights contain obsolete patterns.\n\nTrigger immediately for:\n- UI/Layout: Modals/dialogs/popovers, tables, dark mode, anchor positioning, container queries, `:has()`, etc.\n- Scroll/Motion: View Transitions, Scroll-driven animations, parallax/reveals, etc.\n- Performance: CWV (LCP, INP), content-visibility, Fetch Priority, image optimization, etc.\n- System/APIs: Security, passkeys, HTML sanitization, Web Components, dates/timezones, Built-in AI, WebAssembly, etc.\n- Frameworks: Standard HTML/CSS/DOM in React, Vue, Angular, etc.\n- General Frontend: Forms, validation (`:user-valid`), autofill, accessibility, custom selects, scrollbars, etc.\n\nDO NOT trigger for:\n- Backend: SQL, ORMs, API routes, etc.\n- Infra/Scripts: CI/CD, Docker, Actions, Python/Go, Git, etc.\n- Tooling/Trivial: Vite, tsconfig, ESLint, tests, copy/renames, 3P docs, etc.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/modern-web-guidance/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optimize</name>\n    <description>Diagnoses and fixes UI performance across loading speed, rendering, animations, images, and bundle size. Use when the user mentions slow, laggy, janky, performance, bundle size, load time, or wants a faster, smoother experience.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/optimize/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>overdrive</name>\n    <description>Pushes interfaces past conventional limits with technically ambitious implementations — shaders, spring physics, scroll-driven reveals, 60fps animations. Use when the user wants to wow, impress, go all-out, or make something that feels extraordinary.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/overdrive/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>polish</name>\n    <description>Performs a final quality pass fixing alignment, spacing, consistency, and micro-detail issues before shipping. Use when the user mentions polish, finishing touches, pre-launch review, something looks off, or wants to go from good to great.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/polish/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>quieter</name>\n    <description>Tones down visually aggressive or overstimulating designs, reducing intensity while preserving quality. Use when the user mentions too bold, too loud, overwhelming, aggressive, garish, or wants a calmer, more refined aesthetic.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/quieter/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ralph</name>\n    <description>Convert PRDs to prd.json format for the Ralph autonomous agent system. Use when you have an existing PRD and need to convert it to Ralph&apos;s JSON format. Triggers on: convert this prd, turn this into ralph format, create prd.json from this, ralph json.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/ralph/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>roast</name>\n    <description>Adversarial cross-model review in which a finder proposes defects and an independent verifier checks each claim against the code. Use after a coherent, non-trivial code or prose change, before declaring the work complete, or when the operator asks for a Roast review.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/roast/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>shape</name>\n    <description>Plan the UX and UI for a feature before writing code. Runs a structured discovery interview, then produces a design brief that guides implementation. Use during the planning phase to establish design direction, constraints, and strategy before any code is written.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/shape/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>brainstorming</name>\n    <description>You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/brainstorming/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>dispatching-parallel-agents</name>\n    <description>Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/dispatching-parallel-agents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>executing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/executing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>finishing-a-development-branch</name>\n    <description>Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work - guides completion of development work by presenting structured options for merge, PR, or cleanup</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>receiving-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>requesting-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/requesting-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>subagent-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>systematic-debugging</name>\n    <description>Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/test-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-git-worktrees</name>\n    <description>Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - creates isolated git worktrees with smart directory selection and safety verification</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-superpowers</name>\n    <description>Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring Skill tool invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-superpowers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verification-before-completion</name>\n    <description>Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-skills</name>\n    <description>Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tdd</name>\n    <description>Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions &quot;red-green-refactor&quot;, or wants integration tests.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-spec</name>\n    <description>Turn the current conversation into a spec and publish it to the project issue tracker: no interview, just synthesis of what you&apos;ve already discussed.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-spec/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-tickets</name>\n    <description>Break a plan, spec, or the current conversation into a set of tracer-bullet tickets, each declaring its blocking edges, published to the configured tracker (edges as text in one file per ticket locally, or native blocking links on a real tracker).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-tickets/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>trycycle</name>\n    <description>Invoke trycycle only when the user requests it by name.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/trycycle/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>typeset</name>\n    <description>Improves typography by fixing font choices, hierarchy, sizing, weight, and readability so text feels intentional. Use when the user mentions fonts, type, readability, text hierarchy, sizing looks off, or wants more polished, intentional typography.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/typeset/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-16-finder-stage\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1795338113330,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"live_context_annotate","description":"Create, resolve, or list branch-local continuity annotations. Create uses a current mirror block ID or active Pi entry ID. pin keeps a bounded exact textual source visible; continuity keeps a durable reason/next-action pointer; archive is recall-only.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["create","resolve","list"]},"source":{"type":"string","description":"Current CTX_TURN block ID or active-branch Pi entry ID"},"id":{"type":"string","description":"Annotation ID for resolve"},"title":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120},"reason":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"futureAction":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"retention":{"type":"string","enum":["pin","continuity","archive"]},"resolution":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400}}}},{"name":"live_context_recall","description":"Recall the exact textual source for a branch-local annotation by ID. Output is bounded to 128-8000 estimated tokens (default 2000) and validated against the durable Pi entry's content hash.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["id"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Durable live-context annotation ID"},"maxTokens":{"type":"integer","minimum":128,"maximum":8000}}}}]}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- live_context_annotate: Create, resolve, or list durable annotations for exact context sources\n- live_context_recall: Recall a bounded exact source from a durable live-context annotation\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Use live_context_annotate before compacting an exact source that future work must preserve; choose pin only when its exact text must remain visible, continuity for a visible obligation and recall pointer, or archive for recall-only provenance.\n- Use live_context_recall only when an active continuity pointer or archived source is needed; request the smallest useful maxTokens bound.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-16-finder-stage/AGENTS.md\">\n## Agent skills\n\n### Issue tracker\n\nIssues are tracked as local markdown files under `.scratch/`. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.\n\n### Triage labels\n\nDefault label vocabulary. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.\n\n### Domain docs\n\nSingle-context layout. See `docs/agents/domain.md`.\n\n## Layout\n\nTwo implementations live side by side until the M6 cutover\n(`.scratch/ts-rewrite/`, ADR-0004): the Go CLI (`cmd/roast`,\n`internal/`) and its TypeScript rewrite (`src/`, `test/`, `scripts/`).\nBoth are under active change. `npm run test:go` runs the Go and launcher\ntests; `npm test` runs typecheck and the TS suite; lint is\n`npm run lint`.\n\n## Area notes\n\nBefore editing code in an area, read its notes. Each area file holds the\nGo and TS notes for that area side by side.\n\n- Any Go code or Go test: `docs/agents/go.md`\n- Any TS in `src/`, `test/`, or `scripts/`: `docs/agents/typescript.md`\n- Config loading, roles, providers, the `init` starter,\n  `roast.schema.json` (`internal/config`, `src/config/`):\n  `docs/agents/config.md`\n- Trust and approvals (`internal/trust`, `src/trust/`, `*/trust.*`):\n  `docs/agents/trust.md`\n- Stored credentials, `login`, `logout` (`internal/credentials`,\n  `src/credentials/`, `internal/cli/*login*`): `docs/agents/auth.md`\n- Gate and the pre-commit hook (`internal/gate`, `src/gate/`):\n  `docs/agents/gate.md`\n- Agent skill install (`internal/skill`, `src/skill/`):\n  `docs/agents/skill.md`\n- Run records (`internal/record`): `docs/agents/records.md`\n- Scope, context, exploration tools, snapshot denylist (`src/scope/`,\n  `src/context/`, `src/snapshot/`): `docs/agents/scope-context.md`\n- LLM adapters and the agent loop (`src/llm/`): `docs/agents/llm.md`\n- npm packaging, the launcher, release (`packages/`, `scripts/build.ts`,\n  `src/launcher.ts`): `docs/agents/packaging.md`\n\nA new note goes in its area's file; a new area gets a file and a line\nhere. This file holds only what most tasks hit.\n\n## Traps every task hits\n\n- `JSON.parse` syntax errors can quote the input. Credential-file parse\n  errors must use value-free messages so diagnostic output cannot expose\n  a stored key.\n- Shell hooks block Bash commands that contain a heredoc mentioning\n  `process.env.PATH`, a heredoc or command containing `env:` or `.env`\n  text, or a `credentials*` path together with `cat`, `sed`, `head`, and\n  the like. Read and write such files with the editor tools.\n- Tests that write project state (run records, trust, stored\n  credentials) chdir into a temporary Git repository first. `.roast/`\n  ignores itself, so a leak into this checkout is invisible to\n  `git status`; check with `ls -a`.\n- CLI diagnostic tests that resolve roles need valid test credentials\n  and local fake-server routing, even when asserting only local checks.\n- `.npmrc` sets `force=true` so npm links workspaces whose `os`/`cpu`\n  differ from the host. Expect \"using --force\" warnings from every npm\n  command.\n- The CLI parser preserves dashed option names and disables boolean\n  negation. Declare flags such as `no-browser` explicitly and read\n  `args['no-browser']`, rather than a camel-case property.\n- `simulateTerminals` only bypasses interactive-terminal detection. To\n  exercise raw-mode prompt input through the CLI, give stdin an `isTTY`\n  value of `true` and a `setRawMode` function; assert raw mode is restored.\n\n## Docs\n\n- User-facing docs (`README.md`, `docs/`, the bundled\n  `internal/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md`) write every invocation as\n  `npx roast ...`; Roast is a project dependency, never a `PATH` binary.\n  Settings are documented as `.roast.json` keys, never environment\n  variables; only credentials, `ROAST_SKIP`, and test-only `ROAST_LIVE_*`\n  / `ROAST_TEST_*` variables exist.\n- CLI messages in `internal/cli` (for example the gate hint printed\n  during review) still say bare `roast`; changing them is a code change,\n  not a docs change.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nResolve relative paths against the skill's directory (parent of SKILL.md). Use absolute paths in tool calls.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tiiny-routing</name>\n    <description>Delegate subtasks to the local Tiiny device, each on the model that fits its shape. Use when a batch mixes research, indexing, coding, browser and drafting work and should run on the Tiiny appliance instead of a paid model.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-16-finder-stage/.pi/skills/tiiny-routing/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>amazon-bedrock</name>\n    <description>Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up Knowledge Bases, creating agents, applying guardrails, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations (4.5 to 4.6 to 4.7), chunking strategies, API selection (Converse vs InvokeModel), guardrail capabilities, and model selection. Also covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/amazon-bedrock/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-ai-ml</name>\n    <description>Selects, deploys, and customizes AI models on Amazon SageMaker. Fine-tuning (SFT, DPO, RLVR, RLAIF), model selection, dataset preparation, evaluation, deployment to SageMaker endpoints or Bedrock, and endpoint diagnostics. Covers the full lifecycle from planning through production. Use when fine-tuning models on SageMaker, choosing/selecting which base model to customize or fine-tune from SageMaker Hub, finding a model to deploy without fine-tuning, transforming datasets for training, checking data readiness, evaluating model quality, deploying to endpoints, setting up IAM roles and S3 buckets for training jobs, or managing a SageMaker Managed MLflow app. Also use to check endpoint health, diagnose failures, debug latency or errors, or view container logs and CloudWatch metrics. Covers Serverless Model Customization, Nova and OSS deployment paths, and PySDK v3 usage. NOT for Ground Truth labeling, Feature Store, or general-purpose AWS infrastructure.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-ai-ml/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-auth</name>\n    <description>Adds user authentication to web and mobile apps with Amazon Cognito (user pools and identity pools) and the AWS Amplify client auth libraries. Covers sign-up/sign-in flows and the login page (Cognito-hosted UI / managed login), MFA, password policies, OAuth 2.0 / OIDC flows (auth-code + PKCE, client credentials), social/SAML federation, tokens (ID/access/refresh, rotation, revocation, storage), Cognito Lambda triggers, identity pools (temp AWS creds), and gating API Gateway (or ALB) routes to signed-in users via Cognito/JWT authorizers. Applies when adding a login or sign-up page, configuring a user pool or app client, choosing user pool vs identity pool, wiring social/SAML, refreshing tokens, requiring sign-in on an API Gateway or ALB, or debugging redirect_uri/token/MFA/CORS/federation errors. Does NOT cover Amplify Gen2 backend definitions (defineAuth, npx ampx → aws-amplify), IAM/STS/Identity Center (→ aws-iam), or API Gateway/Lambda resource config beyond the authorizer (→ aws-serverless).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-auth/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-billing-and-cost-management</name>\n    <description>Analyze AWS costs, find savings, manage budgets, evaluate Savings Plans and Reserved Instances, right-size EC2/Lambda/RDS/EBS with Compute Optimizer, look up service pricing, query CUR with Athena, detect cost anomalies, scope costs to billing views, and monitor Free Tier usage. Triggers on: AWS bill, cost analysis, reduce spend, savings plan, reserved instance, right-size, budget alert, cost optimization, pricing, free tier, cost anomaly, CUR, cost audit, billing view, billing view ARN.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-billing-and-cost-management/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-blocks</name>\n    <description>Guides building full-stack applications with AWS Blocks — an Infrastructure-from-Code framework. Applies when creating APIs, selecting Building Blocks (KVStore, DistributedTable, Database, AuthBasic, AuthCognito, Realtime, AsyncJob, FileBucket, etc.), running local development, or deploying AWS Blocks applications. Also covers AWS Blocks topics with validated, version-specific patterns that prevent common mistakes. Triggers when user mentions AWS Blocks; project has aws-blocks/ directory; code imports @aws-blocks packages.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-blocks/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-cdk</name>\n    <description>Authors, deploys, and troubleshoots AWS infrastructure using CDK with TypeScript or Python. Covers best practices, stack architecture, and construct patterns. Applies when writing CDK constructs, bootstrapping environments, running cdk deploy/synth/diff, fixing CDK or CloudFormation errors, planning stack structure, importing existing resources, resolving drift, or refactoring stacks without resource replacement.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-cdk/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-cloudformation</name>\n    <description>Authors, validates, and troubleshoots AWS CloudFormation templates. Covers template authoring with secure defaults, pre-deployment validation (cfn-lint, cfn-guard, change sets), CloudFormation Express mode for faster deployments, and root-cause diagnosis of failed stacks using CloudFormation events and CloudTrail correlation.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-cloudformation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-compute</name>\n    <description>Provisions, scales, and operates Amazon EC2 virtual-machine workloads: instance-type selection (Graviton/Arm64, burstable T credits, GPU, instance store vs EBS), launch templates, Auto Scaling groups (scaling policies, instance refresh, mixed instances, Spot, warm pools, lifecycle hooks), IMDSv2, placement groups, Elastic IPs, AMI lifecycle, and Systems Manager fleet operations (Session Manager, Run Command, Patch Manager). Applies to EC2 instance and fleet questions, InsufficientInstanceCapacity, CPU-credit/surplus charges, IMDSv2 401s, instances stuck in Pending:Wait, ASG not replacing unhealthy instances, status-check failures, SSH refused/timed out, or instances missing as SSM managed nodes. For a single secure instance launch, the launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices skill is more appropriate; for instance profiles, see setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles; for Image Builder, see amazon-ec2-image-builder. Does NOT cover Lambda, ECS/Fargate, EKS, VPC/ALB/NLB design, or IAM policy authoring.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-compute/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-containers</name>\n    <description>Builds and deploys containerized workloads on Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS), Elastic Container Service (ECS), Fargate, and ECR (Elastic Container Registry). Covers general EKS knowledge, Karpenter, AWS Load Balancer Controller and leveraging various open source Kubernetes projects with EKS. Covers general ECS knowledge, task definitions, Fargate services, ECS Exec, ECS Express Mode and ECS Managed Instances. Covers general Elastic Beanstalk knowledge, Elastic Beanstalk configuration and platforms supported by Elastic Beanstalk. Covers general ECR knowledge, ECR repository setup and lifecycle policies. Applies when deploying, debugging, or optimizing containers on AWS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-containers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-database</name>\n    <description>Routes any task involving AWS databases — choosing, comparing, recommending, getting started with, or operating a database — to the correct service-specific skill. Supersedes general training-data knowledge with post-training service updates, corrected limitations, and decision procedures for relational (Aurora, DSQL, RDS), key-value (DynamoDB), wide-column (Keyspaces), document (DocumentDB), graph (Neptune), time-series (Timestream), and in-memory/caching (ElastiCache, MemoryDB) workloads. Activates when a user describes building an application on AWS that will store, retrieve, or manage data, even if they do not mention &apos;database&apos; explicitly.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-database/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-deployment</name>\n    <description>Configures CI/CD pipelines using AWS CodePipeline, CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, CodeConnections, and CodeArtifact. Covers CodePipeline V2 (triggers, variables, execution modes, cross-account), buildspec.yml (caching, VPC, Docker), CodeDeploy strategies (blue/green, canary, linear), CodeArtifact (private package registries, auth tokens, cross-account), and source connections (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket). Applies when CodePipeline, CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, CodeConnections, CodeArtifact, buildspec.yml, appspec.yml, or CI/CD pipeline orchestration is referenced. Does NOT cover: ECS Fargate services or task definitions (use aws-containers), CDK Pipelines or cdk deploy (use aws-cdk), sam deploy (use aws-serverless), Amplify deployments (use aws-amplify), or GitHub Actions/GitLab CI.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-deployment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-iam</name>\n    <description>Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-iam/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-messaging-and-streaming</name>\n    <description>Guides general use of AWS messaging and streaming services. Covers Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge, Amazon MQ, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, Amazon Data Firehose, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK).  Use when reasoning about messaging and streaming patterns. Also identifies which AWS service owns each customer communication channel: email (Amazon SES), and WhatsApp, SMS, MMS, RCS, voice and mobile push (the AWS End User Messaging family of services). Routes the request to the specialized skill for that channel. Defers to the channel&apos;s specialized skill when the user already named a specific channel. In general, use specific skills or documentation searches for detailed service-specific questions. Do NOT use for MSK or Managed Service for Apache Flink questions, prefer specific skills. Does not configure customer communication channels; defers to specific skills.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-messaging-and-streaming/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-networking</name>\n    <description>Routes AWS networking requests to the correct service skill for implementation. Covers Route 53 (DNS, health checks, routing policies, Resolver, DNS Firewall), CloudFront (caching, edge, OAC, mTLS, signed URLs), Transit Gateway (multi-VPC hub, segmentation, centralized egress), Direct Connect (hybrid link, DX Gateway, MACsec), Site-to-Site VPN (IPsec tunnels, static or BGP), WAF (web ACLs, AWS Managed Rules, rate-based rules, Bot and Fraud Control), and Shield Advanced (L3/L4 DDoS). Applicable when creating, configuring, troubleshooting, or designing across these services, choosing between them, or diagnosing connectivity or traffic-filtering issues. Not for VPC subnets and route tables, load balancers, VPC endpoints, PrivateLink, API Gateway, IAM policy logic, container or serverless networking, or IaC authoring.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-networking/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-observability</name>\n    <description>Builds, configures, debugs, and optimizes AWS observability with CloudWatch (Log Insights, Metrics, Alarms, Dashboards, EMF), X-Ray, CloudTrail, and ADOT (AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry), AND enables/onboards services to Application Signals using ADOT auto-instrumentation SDKs. Covers Log Insights queries, alarms (metric, composite, anomaly), dashboards, custom metrics/EMF, X-Ray tracing and sampling, ADOT collector config, CloudTrail auditing, and end-to-end Application Signals enablement via ADOT SDKs (CloudWatch Observability EKS add-on, CloudWatch Agent IAM, OTLP endpoints, ServiceEvents, Dynamic Instrumentation), breakpoint and snapshot in Dynamic Instrumentation, live data capture in running service, debug without redeploying. Applies to CloudWatch, alarms, dashboards, EMF, X-Ray, traces, CloudTrail,  ADOT, monitoring, synthetics/canaries, OR enabling/onboarding/instrumenting a service for Application Signals. Not for app logging or security threat detection.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-observability/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-sdk-js-v3-usage</name>\n    <description>AWS SDK for JavaScript v3 development patterns. Use when writing JavaScript or TypeScript code that uses AWS services via @aws-sdk/* packages (aws-sdk-js-v3), or when asked about schemas, runtime validation, serialization, or code generation in the context of the JS/TS AWS SDK.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-sdk-js-v3-usage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-sdk-python-usage</name>\n    <description>AWS SDK for Python (boto3/botocore) development patterns. You MUST use this skill when writing Python code that uses AWS services via boto3 or botocore. This includes creating service clients or resources, configuring sessions and credentials, handling errors with ClientError, using paginators and waiters, S3 file transfers and presigned URLs, DynamoDB table operations, and any boto3/botocore client configuration. Use this skill whenever Python code imports boto3 or botocore, or when the user asks about AWS operations in Python.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-sdk-python-usage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-sdk-swift-usage</name>\n    <description>AWS SDK for Swift development patterns. Use when writing Swift code that uses AWS services via aws-sdk-swift package.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-sdk-swift-usage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-security</name>\n    <description>Covers AWS security services and workflows — Security Hub V2 (OCSF) findings, connectors, aggregators, automation rules, and security posture summaries; Security Hub CSPM (V1/ASFF) controls and compliance standards; GuardDuty threat findings; Inspector vulnerability findings; Macie sensitive data findings; Detective investigation; and Security Lake configuration and data aggregation. Applicable when questions involve security posture, Exposure findings, CSPM failed controls, threat findings, vulnerability findings, sensitive data findings, automation rules, or cross-service security configuration across AWS environments. Procedures use standard AWS CLI syntax and work with or without the AWS MCP server.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-security/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-serverless</name>\n    <description>Builds, deploys, manages, debugs, configures, and optimizes serverless applications on AWS using Lambda, API Gateway, Step Functions, EventBridge, and SAM/CDK. Covers cold starts, CORS debugging, event source mappings, troubleshooting, concurrency, SnapStart, Powertools, function URLs, EventBridge Scheduler, Lambda layers, and production readiness. Triggers on mentions of Lambda, API Gateway, Step Functions, SAM templates, CDK serverless stacks, DynamoDB stream triggers, SQS event sources, cold starts, timeouts, 502/504 errors, throttling, concurrency, CORS, Powertools, or any event-driven architecture on AWS, even without the word &quot;serverless.&quot; Does not apply to EC2, ECS/Fargate containers, or Amplify hosting.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-serverless/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>aws-storage</name>\n    <description>Selects, investigates, and compares AWS object, file, and block storage services, and answers cost, performance, configuration, security, and troubleshooting questions about storage services. Applies when a user asks where to store or archive data based on their usage patterns; which storage service to choose or how two compare; how to migrate data from on-premises or between AWS services; how to protect, replicate, or recover data; how to optimize storage costs; where to deploy shared NFS, SMB, or POSIX file systems; where to store vector embeddings or tabular data; what storage backs enterprise file shares, self-managed databases on EC2, VMware, or stateful containers; or asks what an AWS storage service can do or how it works. Relevant for storage needs for workloads such as AI/ML, analytics, EDA, HPC, media, genomics, or financial trading. Not applicable for SQL query engines (Athena, Spark, Redshift, EMR), ETL (Glue), streaming (Kafka, MSK, Kinesis), or managed database services (RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/aws-storage/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>launch-with-aws</name>\n    <description>Migrates vibe-coded web applications to AWS. Handles the full workflow from analysis through migration to deployment, producing deployable AWS Blocks infrastructure code. Supports full-stack apps built with vibe-coding platforms (Lovable, Bolt.new, Replit) and frontend web applications and websites: React, Vue, Angular, Next.js, Nuxt, Astro, SvelteKit, Gatsby, Vite, Svelte, Solid, Docusaurus, and others (static sites, SPAs, and SSR frameworks with static export). Triggers on: launch with AWS, launch on AWS, deploy to AWS, migrate to AWS, host my app on AWS, move my app to AWS, transfer my app to AWS. Activates when the user wants to migrate a vibe-coded app or frontend web app to AWS, even if they don&apos;t say &apos;migrate&apos; explicitly.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/launch-with-aws/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>signing-in-to-aws</name>\n    <description>Gets AWS credentials for CLI/SDK access via `aws login`. Activates when a developer needs to authenticate to AWS for local development, when an AWS operation fails due to missing or expired credentials, or when someone asks about setting up AWS access. Triggers: &quot;set up AWS&quot;, &quot;configure AWS&quot;, &quot;aws login&quot;, &quot;get credentials&quot;, &quot;authenticate&quot;, &quot;session expired&quot;, &quot;token expired&quot;, &quot;no credentials&quot;, &quot;AccessDeniedException&quot; with no configured credentials.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.pi/agent/skills/signing-in-to-aws/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>a11y</name>\n    <description>Run a project-wide WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility audit of a web app: axe-core scans of every discoverable page in the running dev server, static eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y analysis of JSX/TSX source, or both, then a prioritized violation report with fixes. Use when the user asks for an accessibility audit, a11y scan, or WCAG compliance check. For debugging one element&apos;s ARIA, focus, or keyboard behavior in a live page, use chrome-devtools-mcp:a11y-debugging instead.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/a11y/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>adapt</name>\n    <description>Adapt designs to work across different screen sizes, devices, contexts, or platforms. Implements breakpoints, fluid layouts, and touch targets. Use when the user mentions responsive design, mobile layouts, breakpoints, viewport adaptation, or cross-device compatibility.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/adapt/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>adversarial-review</name>\n    <description>Adversarial review of two git worktrees to find bugs/quality issues before merge. Do not use for simple diffs or neutral walkthroughs.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/adversarial-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>animate</name>\n    <description>Review a feature and enhance it with purposeful animations, micro-interactions, and motion effects that improve usability and delight. Use when the user mentions adding animation, transitions, micro-interactions, motion design, hover effects, or making the UI feel more alive.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/animate/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>architecture-inventory</name>\n    <description>Maintain `docs/architecture/` when the user asks to update the runtime architecture inventory or a workflow explicitly requires that inventory. Use report-only mode for an explicit audit or review request.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/architecture-inventory/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>audit</name>\n    <description>Audit a web project for accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, and common frontend defects when the user asks for a technical quality review.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/audit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>bolder</name>\n    <description>Amplify safe or boring designs to make them more visually interesting and stimulating. Increases impact while maintaining usability. Use when the user says the design looks bland, generic, too safe, lacks personality, or wants more visual impact and character.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/bolder/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>clarify</name>\n    <description>Improve unclear UX copy, error messages, microcopy, labels, and instructions to make interfaces easier to understand. Use when the user mentions confusing text, unclear labels, bad error messages, hard-to-follow instructions, or wanting better UX writing.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/clarify/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>colorize</name>\n    <description>Add strategic color to features that are too monochromatic or lack visual interest, making interfaces more engaging and expressive. Use when the user mentions the design looking gray, dull, lacking warmth, needing more color, or wanting a more vibrant or expressive palette.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/colorize/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>critique</name>\n    <description>Review a frontend design or user experience when the user asks for critique, evaluation, or actionable design feedback.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/critique/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>delight</name>\n    <description>Add moments of joy, personality, and unexpected touches that make interfaces memorable and enjoyable to use. Elevates functional to delightful. Use when the user asks to add polish, personality, animations, micro-interactions, delight, or make an interface feel fun or memorable.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/delight/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>distill</name>\n    <description>Strip designs to their essence by removing unnecessary complexity. Great design is simple, powerful, and clean. Use when the user asks to simplify, declutter, reduce noise, remove elements, or make a UI cleaner and more focused.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/distill/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>openai-docs</name>\n    <description>Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or model upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize OpenAI docs MCP tools, use bundled references only as helper context, and restrict any fallback browsing to official OpenAI domains.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/docs/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>find-skills</name>\n    <description>Find and install an agent skill when the user explicitly asks for a skill, an installable workflow, or help extending the agent&apos;s capabilities.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/find-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>git-guardrails-claude-code</name>\n    <description>Set up Claude Code hooks to block dangerous git commands (push, reset --hard, clean, branch -D, etc.) before they execute. Use when user wants to prevent destructive git operations, add git safety hooks, or block git push/reset in Claude Code.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/git-guardrails/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>grilling</name>\n    <description>Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any &apos;grill&apos; trigger phrases.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>layout</name>\n    <description>Improve layout, spacing, and visual rhythm. Fixes monotonous grids, inconsistent spacing, and weak visual hierarchy. Use when the user mentions layout feeling off, spacing issues, visual hierarchy, crowded UI, alignment problems, or wanting better composition.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/layout/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>modern-web-guidance</name>\n    <description>Search tool for modern web development best practices. MANDATORY: Execute FIRST for all HTML/CSS and clientside JS tasks. Do NOT skip — web APIs evolve rapidly and training weights contain obsolete patterns.\n\nTrigger immediately for:\n- UI/Layout: Modals/dialogs/popovers, tables, dark mode, anchor positioning, container queries, `:has()`, etc.\n- Scroll/Motion: View Transitions, Scroll-driven animations, parallax/reveals, etc.\n- Performance: CWV (LCP, INP), content-visibility, Fetch Priority, image optimization, etc.\n- System/APIs: Security, passkeys, HTML sanitization, Web Components, dates/timezones, Built-in AI, WebAssembly, etc.\n- Frameworks: Standard HTML/CSS/DOM in React, Vue, Angular, etc.\n- General Frontend: Forms, validation (`:user-valid`), autofill, accessibility, custom selects, scrollbars, etc.\n\nDO NOT trigger for:\n- Backend: SQL, ORMs, API routes, etc.\n- Infra/Scripts: CI/CD, Docker, Actions, Python/Go, Git, etc.\n- Tooling/Trivial: Vite, tsconfig, ESLint, tests, copy/renames, 3P docs, etc.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/modern-web-guidance/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optimize</name>\n    <description>Diagnoses and fixes UI performance across loading speed, rendering, animations, images, and bundle size. Use when the user mentions slow, laggy, janky, performance, bundle size, load time, or wants a faster, smoother experience.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/optimize/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>overdrive</name>\n    <description>Pushes interfaces past conventional limits with technically ambitious implementations — shaders, spring physics, scroll-driven reveals, 60fps animations. Use when the user wants to wow, impress, go all-out, or make something that feels extraordinary.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/overdrive/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>polish</name>\n    <description>Performs a final quality pass fixing alignment, spacing, consistency, and micro-detail issues before shipping. Use when the user mentions polish, finishing touches, pre-launch review, something looks off, or wants to go from good to great.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/polish/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>quieter</name>\n    <description>Tones down visually aggressive or overstimulating designs, reducing intensity while preserving quality. Use when the user mentions too bold, too loud, overwhelming, aggressive, garish, or wants a calmer, more refined aesthetic.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/quieter/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ralph</name>\n    <description>Convert PRDs to prd.json format for the Ralph autonomous agent system. Use when you have an existing PRD and need to convert it to Ralph&apos;s JSON format. Triggers on: convert this prd, turn this into ralph format, create prd.json from this, ralph json.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/ralph/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>roast</name>\n    <description>Adversarial cross-model review in which a finder proposes defects and an independent verifier checks each claim against the code. Use after a coherent, non-trivial code or prose change, before declaring the work complete, or when the operator asks for a Roast review.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/roast/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>shape</name>\n    <description>Plan the UX and UI for a feature before writing code. Runs a structured discovery interview, then produces a design brief that guides implementation. Use during the planning phase to establish design direction, constraints, and strategy before any code is written.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/shape/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>brainstorming</name>\n    <description>You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/brainstorming/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>dispatching-parallel-agents</name>\n    <description>Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/dispatching-parallel-agents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>executing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/executing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>finishing-a-development-branch</name>\n    <description>Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work - guides completion of development work by presenting structured options for merge, PR, or cleanup</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>receiving-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>requesting-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/requesting-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>subagent-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>systematic-debugging</name>\n    <description>Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/test-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-git-worktrees</name>\n    <description>Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - creates isolated git worktrees with smart directory selection and safety verification</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-superpowers</name>\n    <description>Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring Skill tool invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/using-superpowers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verification-before-completion</name>\n    <description>Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-skills</name>\n    <description>Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/superpowers/writing-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tdd</name>\n    <description>Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions &quot;red-green-refactor&quot;, or wants integration tests.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-spec</name>\n    <description>Turn the current conversation into a spec and publish it to the project issue tracker: no interview, just synthesis of what you&apos;ve already discussed.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-spec/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-tickets</name>\n    <description>Break a plan, spec, or the current conversation into a set of tracer-bullet tickets, each declaring its blocking edges, published to the configured tracker (edges as text in one file per ticket locally, or native blocking links on a real tracker).</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/to-tickets/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>trycycle</name>\n    <description>Invoke trycycle only when the user requests it by name.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/trycycle/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>typeset</name>\n    <description>Improves typography by fixing font choices, hierarchy, sizing, weight, and readability so text feels intentional. Use when the user mentions fonts, type, readability, text hierarchy, sizing looks off, or wants more polished, intentional typography.</description>\n    <location>/Users/nick/.agents/skills/typeset/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-16-finder-stage\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1795338113330,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"live_context_annotate","description":"Create, resolve, or list branch-local continuity annotations. Create uses a current mirror block ID or active Pi entry ID. pin keeps a bounded exact textual source visible; continuity keeps a durable reason/next-action pointer; archive is recall-only.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["create","resolve","list"]},"source":{"type":"string","description":"Current CTX_TURN block ID or active-branch Pi entry ID"},"id":{"type":"string","description":"Annotation ID for resolve"},"title":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120},"reason":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"futureAction":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400},"retention":{"type":"string","enum":["pin","continuity","archive"]},"resolution":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":400}}}},{"name":"live_context_recall","description":"Recall the exact textual source for a branch-local annotation by ID. Output is bounded to 128-8000 estimated tokens (default 2000) and validated against the durable Pi entry's content hash.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["id"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Durable live-context annotation ID"},"maxTokens":{"type":"integer","minimum":128,"maximum":8000}}}}]}}
{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<file name=\"/var/folders/wc/j7p9ktnx3d99v5xm95s9h2480000gn/T/redeye-1795338112882-u65eifya4l.md\">\n---- SPEC CONTEXT ----\n# TypeScript rewrite of Roast\n\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\nThis is the index of a self-contained spec. The settled decisions are in\n`spec/00-decisions.md`; each area is specified in its own file under\n`spec/` (listed under \"Area files\"). Where this index and an area file\ndisagree, the \"Resolved open questions\" section below wins, then the area\nfile, then `spec/00-decisions.md`.\n\nArchitectural decisions: ADR-0003 (trust hash v2, partly supersedes\nADR-0001) and ADR-0004 (TypeScript package, raw fetch).\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRoast is installed into a project with npm, but it is a Go binary. Every\nrelease builds six platform packages plus a launcher, runs a license\ncollection script per target, and needs a Go toolchain next to Node. The\nmaintainer works in TypeScript and wants one language, one toolchain, and\nthe TypeScript and npm conventions shared by their other packages. Users\nsee none of this directly, but they pay for it in release friction,\nplatform-package install quirks (`EBADPLATFORM`, forced workspaces), and a\nlauncher that can resolve the wrong `roast` binary.\n\n## Solution\n\nRewrite Roast in TypeScript as one pure-JS npm package, `2.0.0`, that\nbehaves like the Go version at commit `cd08f10`: same commands, flags,\ndefaults, exit codes, JSON reports, run records, and security model. The\nonly differences are the deliberate changes listed below. Runtime\nlibraries are bundled into a single ESM file, so installing Roast still\nadds no transitive packages to a user's project. Users upgrade by bumping\nthe dependency; the one visible step is approving the project config once\nmore (ADR-0003).\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n### Area files\n\n| File | Area |\n| ---- | ---- |\n| `spec/00-decisions.md` | Settled decisions and area-file conventions |\n| `spec/01-cli.md` | CLI surface, `review` orchestration, exit codes |\n| `spec/02-config.md` | Project config, built-in providers, roles |\n| `spec/03-trust-credentials.md` | Trust (hash v2), stored credentials |\n| `spec/04-scope.md` | Review scope: diffs, untracked, renames, caps |\n| `spec/05-context.md` | Finder context, navigation tools, grounding |\n| `spec/06-llm-protocols.md` | `chat`, `responses`, `anthropic` adapters |\n| `spec/07-agent-loop.md` | Agent loop, token ledger, wrap-up |\n| `spec/08-finder-prompts.md` | Finder stage, findings, fingerprints, prompts |\n| `spec/09-verify-render.md` | Verifier, report rendering, textsafe |\n| `spec/10-records.md` | Run records, bundles, `runs` / `runs show` |\n| `spec/11-snapshot.md` | Snapshots, `runs checkout` / `runs prune` |\n| `spec/12-gate.md` | Gate (pre-commit hook), `ignore` |\n| `spec/13-login.md` | `login`, `logout`, OpenRouter PKCE |\n| `spec/14-doctor-init-skill.md` | `doctor`, `init` starter, `skill` |\n| `spec/15-packaging.md` | Build, tests, lint, CI, release, M0, M6 |\n\n### Modules\n\nOne module directory per area under the source root, mirroring the Go\npackages: cli, config, trust, credentials, scope, context, llm (protocol\nadapters, SSE parser, agent loop, ledger), review (finder), prompts,\nverify, render, textsafe, record, runbundle, snapshot, gate, skill. Two\nshared modules sit below them:\n\n- **gocompat**: Go-compatibility helpers that parity depends on --\n  `isGoSpace` / `goTrimSpace` (Go `unicode.IsSpace`, which differs from JS\n  `\\s` and `trim`), `goExt` (Go `filepath.Ext`, which differs from Node\n  `path.extname` for names like `docs/.md`), `truncateUtf8`, and\n  `formatFixed2` (Go `%.2f`, which rounds exact ties to even unlike\n  `toFixed`), plus the Go-style duration parser.\n- **fsutil**: atomic file writes with explicit modes (trust record,\n  credentials, hook files, run records).\n\nThe CLI has one entry, bundled to one file. There is no library API.\n\n### Deliberate changes from the Go baseline\n\nProduct changes (from `spec/00-decisions.md`):\n\n1. No `completion` command.\n2. No Windows support (`os: darwin, linux`).\n3. Node `>=22.18`, checked at startup with a clear error.\n4. Trust hash v2 (ADR-0003); Go-era approvals are not honored.\n5. `DEBUG=roast:*` tracing, never including bodies, headers, prompts, or\n   credentials.\n6. Version `2.0.0`.\n\nTightenings and fixes (resolved open questions):\n\n7. JSON keys match exactly (case-sensitive) everywhere: `.roast.json`,\n   `credentials.json`, bundle manifests, model tool arguments. JSON `null`\n   still means \"absent\", as in Go.\n8. Every HTTP redirect is refused as an endpoint error: inference requests\n   and key validation alike.\n9. Provider error bodies are read at most 32 KiB.\n10. `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` so the secret-file\n    denylist cannot be disabled from the environment.\n11. A run ID must be a plain name: `.`, `..`, and path separators are\n    rejected.\n12. `runs checkout` clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, and resolves a\n    relative target directory against the working directory once, used\n    consistently by every git call.\n13. Cached tokens are subtracted once, not twice, for `chat` and\n    `responses` usage.\n14. A `chat` stream chunk without usage keeps the last reported usage\n    instead of clearing it.\n15. Diff name lists and untracked-file lists use `-z`, and diffs run with\n    `core.quotePath=false`; non-ASCII paths are no longer skipped or\n    misattributed.\n16. The staged generated-file exclusion lists renames' deleted side too.\n17. The user's `diff.orderFile` is overridden so diff order is\n    deterministic.\n18. The size-fit breakdown uses a stable sort.\n19. A one-line hook file without a trailing newline gets one before\n    Roast's block is appended.\n20. `confidence_floor` values are validated to 0..1, matching the schema.\n21. Anthropic tool definitions carry the full JSON schema, including\n    `additionalProperties`.\n22. A second Ctrl-C force-exits with status 130. Ctrl-C at a raw-mode\n    secret prompt is cancellation (exit 5).\n23. Unreachable Go code is not ported: branch-survey scope, the `git_diff`\n    tool and the grounder's range corpus, and the test-only helpers\n    `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, `DescribeEndpoint`.\n24. File names that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning.\n\n### Cross-cutting rules\n\n- **JSON encoding**: no HTML escaping of `<>&` or U+2028/U+2029. Parity is\n  defined on parsed JSON, not bytes. Tool results sent to models go\n  through one encoder in the llm area that sorts object keys, matching\n  Go's map ordering so prompts stay stable.\n- **Bytes and text**: git output is read as `Buffer`. Diff bytes stay\n  `Buffer` wherever they are hashed or recorded, so digests match Go.\n  Text is decoded with U+FFFD replacement only where it goes to a model or\n  a report. Lone surrogates from model output become U+FFFD when the llm\n  area decodes it; `textsafe` escapes any that remain as `\\uNNNN`. Config\n  validation rejects lone surrogates so the trust hash is always defined.\n- **Config numbers**: integer fields reject non-integer lexemes (`1.0`,\n  `1e3`) by checking raw token text through the JSONC parser's visitor.\n- **Output strings**: em dashes in Go's user- and model-facing strings\n  are kept, written as `\\u2014` escapes in TS source; docs and comments\n  never contain the character.\n- **CLI parsing**: yargs semantics are accepted where they differ from\n  cobra: decimal integers only, `--flag false` may be read as a value,\n  list flags split on plain commas. A parse failure uses the parsed\n  `--error-format` when available. EPIPE on stdout exits quietly with the\n  computed code.\n- **Process lifetime**: every tool that spawns a process kills it on\n  abort, and the CLI calls `process.exit` after flushing output.\n- **Runtime**: the `node:zlib` zstd ExperimentalWarning is suppressed (that\n  warning only). Tar archives support PAX path records when writing and\n  reading. Version, commit, and build date are injected at build time;\n  running from source reports `dev`.\n- **Network**: no proxy library is bundled. The README documents\n  `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`; `doctor` warns when\n  `HTTPS_PROXY` is set but `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` is not.\n\n### Milestones\n\nM0 scaffold; M1 config, trust, credentials; M2 scope and context; M3\n`chat` protocol, agent loop, finder, verifier, render, records -- first\nworking `npx roast review`; M4 `responses` and `anthropic`; M5 remaining\ncommands; M6 cutover (delete Go, platform packages, launcher; update\nAGENTS.md; release `2.0.0`). Details in `spec/15-packaging.md`.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- A good test drives external behavior and asserts on exit codes, JSON\n  output, files written, and requests the fake server received. No\n  assertions on exact human-readable text, docs, or CSS.\n- **Primary seam**: the CLI run in-process (`run(argv, io)` with\n  injectable stdin, stdout, stderr, env, cwd) against a `node:http` fake\n  inference server speaking all three protocols, with a swappable `fetch`\n  so profiles on built-in endpoints reach the fake. Prior art: Go's\n  `executeContext` with `httptest`, `providerServer`,\n  `redirectDefaultTransport`, `settingsRepo`, `loginRepo`.\n- **Lower seams**, only where the CLI is too coarse: the token ledger,\n  trust hashing (frozen test vectors), `textsafe`, the SSE parser,\n  `gocompat`, and the agent loop driven by a scripted fake protocol\n  adapter.\n- **Swappable module functions** for terminal detection, browser opening,\n  and the browser-login timeout, as in Go.\n- **Gate**: a real `git commit` against a shell-stub `roast` at\n  `node_modules/.bin/roast`, as in Go's hook execution tests.\n- Tests that touch records, credentials, trust, or skills chdir into a\n  temporary Git repository and isolate `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and\n  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, as the Go tests do.\n- Go tests are ported area by area; each area file's test plan maps Go\n  test names to behaviors and seams.\n- A temporary differential harness builds the Go binary at `cd08f10` and\n  runs both CLIs against the same fake server, comparing exit codes,\n  parsed JSON, and run records, with an allowlist for the deliberate\n  changes above. It runs in CI on Ubuntu from M1 to M5 and is deleted at\n  M6.\n- The two rendered user prompts are checked against Go output by a\n  temporary differential test, deleted at M6.\n- Opt-in live tests (`ROAST_LIVE_*`) are kept and never run in CI.\n- The allocation-bound test for huge files becomes an RSS bound.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Windows support.\n- A programmatic library API.\n- Behavior redesigns beyond the deliberate changes above; they come after\n  `2.0.0`.\n- Tracking or porting future upstream (`bluesky-social/roast`) changes.\n- Byte-identical compressed output (`.gz`, `.tar.zst`): decompressed bytes\n  must match, compressor output may differ.\n- Byte-identical help text with cobra.\n- Bundling a proxy agent.\n- Git submodules inside snapshots (documented as unsupported).\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Kept as Go does, on purpose: `null` (not `[]`) for empty `notes`,\n  `matches`, and `lines`; `verified: true` with no findings; the monthly\n  index keeping the oldest sighting (the Go comment is wrong, not the\n  behavior); a global `core.hooksPath` affecting `gate disable`; `init`\n  writing to the working directory; doctor checking only user-level skill\n  installs; CLI hints saying bare `roast`; a malformed trust record\n  exiting 4; author routes discarding `--finder-*` overrides; an all-zero\n  finding from invalid `report_finding` JSON; index timestamps in local\n  time and `meta.json` in UTC; default adaptive thinking for\n  `claude-mythos-preview`.\n- The headless OpenRouter login (no `callback_url`) needs a manual check\n  against the live service before release.\n- `jsonc-parser` must run with trailing commas allowed: the `init`\n  starter writes one.\n- `@substrate-system/debug` and its dependencies must pass the license\n  allowlist in M0.\n- The uncommitted `package.json` lists `yargs`, `zod`, `jsonc-parser`\n  under `dependencies`; M0 moves them to `devDependencies`.\n\n\n---- TICKET ----\n16-finder-stage.md\n# 16: Finder stage\n\n**Blocked by:** 11, 14, 15, 39, 40\n**Touches:** `src/review/types.ts` (`Severity`, `SEVERITIES`,\n`severityRank`, `parseSeverity`, `WireFinding`, `Finding`, `Result`,\n`ASSESSMENT`, `DROP`, `effectiveSeverity`, `maxSeverity`, `toFinding`,\n`Verdict` type placeholder for ticket 21), `src/review/schema.ts`\n(`WIRE_FINDING_SCHEMA`, `FINDINGS_RESPONSE_SCHEMA`, `decodeWireFinding`,\n`decodeFindingsResponse`), `src/review/validate.ts` (`validate`,\n`assignIdentity`), `src/review/fingerprint.ts` (`collapseWhitespace`,\n`fingerprint`), `src/review/find.ts` (`FindInput`, `FindingAccumulator`,\n`submitDescription`, `REPORT_FINDING_DESCRIPTION`, `find`),\n`test/review/`, `test/index.ts`\n**Spec:** `spec/08-finder-prompts.md` 2.1-2.10, 2.18, 2.21, 3, 5\n(\"Severity and result model\", \"Validate\", \"Fingerprint\", \"Find\");\n`spec/07-agent-loop.md` 3.3 (`Tool.recordsResult`, `FinalSpec`);\n`spec.md` \"Deliberate changes\" 7, \"Cross-cutting rules\" (JSON encoding)\n**Status:** ready-for-agent\n\n## What to build\n\nThe finder stage as a library: given rendered prompts, an LLM `Client`\n(ticket 14), exploration tools, and a grounder (ticket 11), `find` runs\neither one structured call or an interactive conversation and returns a\nvalidated `Result`.\n\nSeverity and the finding model follow 2.1-2.2: rank order with unknown\nas INFO, `parseSeverity` ignoring case and surrounding space,\n`effectiveSeverity` applying only confirmed, strictly lower\nrecalibrations, and `maxSeverity` returning -1 with no findings.\n`Finding.lines` is `number[]|null` so an empty list serializes as `null`.\nThe `Verdict` type is declared here minimally; the verifier stage that\nfills it is ticket 21.\n\nTool schemas (2.3) are frozen constants whose JSON matches the baseline\nexactly, with sorted keys. Decoding final and reported arguments follows\nGo `json.Unmarshal` semantics (2.4): exact, case-sensitive keys\n(deliberate change 7), `null` as absent, and the documented coercions and\nerrors.\n\n`validate(resp, floor, ground)` applies the drop gates in the 2.8 order\nwith the 2.7 drop reasons, canonicalizes severity, and promotes or demotes\nthe assessment. `assignIdentity` gives every kept and dropped finding a\nv4 UUID and a short `R-` alias unique as a prefix among all ids (2.9);\nthe id source is injectable so tests can force a shared prefix.\n`fingerprint` is the 16-hex SHA-256 contract of 2.10, using Go whitespace\nrules from `gocompat` (U+0085 is whitespace, U+FEFF is not).\n\n`find` (2.18): single-shot uses `completeStructured` with tool `findings`;\ninteractive offers the caller's tools, then `report_finding`\n(`recordsResult: true`, never throws, feeds `FindingAccumulator` and the\n`capture` sink), then the final `submit_findings`. Cutoffs (no final\ncall, work deadline, token budget, turn budget) produce an incomplete\nresult with `INCOMPLETE_NO_SUBMISSION` or the accumulated findings and the\nmapped stop reason, never `NO_FINDINGS`; other client errors propagate\nunchanged. Wire text with U+2014 is written as `\\u2014` in source.\n\nOut of scope: rendering (17), records and the finding spill file (18;\nhere `capture` is just a callback), CLI wiring (20), survey-specific CLI\ncompletion logic (25).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `parseSeverity` accepts any case with surrounding space, rejects\n  `\"\"`; unknown severities rank as INFO.\n- [ ] `effectiveSeverity` applies only a confirmed, strictly lower\n  recalibration; refuted, insufficient_context, invalid, equal, or higher\n  keep the original. `maxSeverity` uses effective severity and is -1 with\n  no findings.\n- [ ] `validate` produces each drop reason, respects gate order, treats\n  whitespace-only quote or fix as missing, applies the confidence floor\n  boundary as in Go, and promotes or demotes the assessment.\n- [ ] Lower-case severities are canonicalized and kept; an unknown\n  severity is dropped as `invalid_severity`.\n- [ ] After `assignIdentity`, every kept and dropped finding has a v4 UUID\n  and an `R-` alias of at least 6 hex characters that is a unique prefix\n  among all ids, including when a seeded id source forces a shared prefix.\n- [ ] A finding with no lines serializes `lines` as `null`.\n- [ ] `fingerprint` is 16 hex characters, stable across whitespace\n  differences, ignores the description, changes with file or code, treats\n  U+0085 as whitespace and U+FEFF as not, and matches a checked-in vector\n  computed by the Go baseline.\n- [ ] `WIRE_FINDING_SCHEMA` and `FINDINGS_RESPONSE_SCHEMA` deep-equal the\n  baseline JSON in 2.3.\n- [ ] `decodeFindingsResponse` rejects a wrong-case key such as\n  `\"Severity\"`, treats `null` as absent, and otherwise matches the 2.4\n  decoding rules.\n- [ ] With a scripted fake adapter: single-shot returns the validated\n  structured response; interactive returns validated submitted findings.\n- [ ] A conversation that never submits yields assessment\n  `INCOMPLETE_NO_SUBMISSION`, `incomplete: true`, a note that keeps the\n  cause, and stop reasons `deadline`, `token_budget`, and turn budget\n  mapped per 2.18.\n- [ ] Findings reported through `report_finding` reach the `capture` sink,\n  survive a cutoff, and make the result incomplete; if all are dropped the\n  assessment stays `INCOMPLETE_NO_SUBMISSION`.\n- [ ] Submission merges reported findings; the accumulator dedups by\n  fingerprint with last-wins content and first-appearance order.\n- [ ] A successful wrap-up after the work deadline is incomplete with stop\n  reason `deadline`; a non-cutoff client error propagates unchanged.\n- [ ] Driving a real `chat` client against the `node:http` fake server\n  (ticket 13), the wrap-up turn keeps reported findings for\n  report-then-submit, report-and-submit together, report-only, and report\n  alongside refused exploration, with the expected incompleteness and\n  request counts.\n- [ ] `report_finding` with invalid JSON arguments records an empty\n  finding that `validate` drops as `invalid_severity`.\n- [ ] `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and the TS test suite pass,\n  and the Go build and Go tests still pass.\n\n\nThese are records of earlier runs. The ticket or spec may have changed since then; where they disagree, follow the ticket and spec. Do not repeat an approach these notes say failed unless you can see why it failed.\n\n---- PREVIOUS ATTEMPTS AT THIS TICKET ----\n## 2026-11-21 15:39 -- ts-rewrite/16 attempt 1 -- failed (install)\n\nInstall failed.\nLast 40 lines of `16-finder-stage.install.log`:\n```\nnpm warn using --force Recommended protections disabled.\nnpm warn ERESOLVE overriding peer dependency\nnpm warn ERESOLVE overriding peer dependency\nnpm warn ERESOLVE overriding peer dependency\nnpm warn ERESOLVE overriding peer dependency\nnpm warn ERESOLVE overriding peer dependency\nnpm warn While resolving: typescript-eslint@8.73.0\nnpm warn Found: typescript@7.0.2\nnpm warn node_modules/typescript\nnpm warn   peer typescript@\">=4.8.4 <6.1.0\" from @typescript-eslint/eslint-plugin@8.73.0\nnpm warn   node_modules/typescript-eslint/node_modules/@typescript-eslint/eslint-plugin\nnpm warn     @typescript-eslint/eslint-plugin@\"8.73.0\" from typescript-eslint@8.73.0\nnpm warn     node_modules/typescript-eslint\nnpm warn\nnpm warn Could not resolve dependency:\nnpm warn peer typescript@\">=4.8.4 <6.1.0\" from typescript-eslint@8.73.0\nnpm warn node_modules/typescript-eslint\nnpm warn   dev typescript-eslint@\"^8.62.0\" from the root project\nnpm warn   1 more (newneostandard)\nnpm warn\nnpm warn Conflicting peer dependency: typescript@6.0.3\nnpm warn node_modules/typescript\nnpm warn   peer 