{"type":"entry_appended","entry":{"type":"custom","customType":"pi.virtual-model-state","data":{"provider":"tiiny-router","modelId":"auto","state":{"tier":"research","goal":"<file name=\"/var/folders/wc/j7p9ktnx3d99v5xm95s9h2480000gn/T/redeye-1795338112882-u65eifya4l.md\"> ---- SPEC CONTEXT ---- # TypeScript rewrite of Roast **Status:** ready-for-agent This is the index of a self-contained spec. The settled decisions are in `spec/00-decisions.md`; each area is specified in its own file under `spec/` (listed under \"Area files\"). Where this index and an area file disagree, the \"Resolved open questions\" section below wins, then the area file, then `spec/00-decisions.md`. Architectural decisions: ADR-0003 (trust hash v2, partly supersedes ADR-0001) and ADR-0004 (TypeScript package, raw fetch). ## Problem Statement Roast is installed into a project with npm, but it is a Go binary. Every release builds six platform packages plus a launcher, runs a license collection script per target, and needs a Go toolchain next to Node. The maintainer works in TypeScript and wants one language, one toolchain, and the TypeScript and npm conventions shared by their other packages. Users see none of this directly, but they pay for it in release friction, platform-package install quirks (`EBADPLATFORM`, forced workspaces), and a launcher that can resolve the wrong `roast` binary. ## Solution Rewrite Roast in TypeScript as one pure-JS npm package, `2.0.0`, that behaves like the Go version at commit `cd08f10`: same commands, flags, defaults, exit codes, JSON reports, run records, and security model. The only differences are the deliberate changes listed below. Runtime libraries are bundled into a single ESM file, so installing Roast still adds no transitive packages to a user's project. Users upgrade by bumping the dependency; the one visible step is approving the project config once more (ADR-0003). ## Implementation Decisions ### Area files | File | Area | | ---- | ---- | | `spec/00-decisions.md` | Settled decisions and area-file conventions | | `spec/01-cli.md` | CLI surface, `review` orchestration, exit codes | | `spec/02-config.md` | Project config, built-in providers, roles | | `spec/03-trust-credentials.md` | Trust (hash v2), stored credentials | | `spec/04-scope.md` | Review scope: diffs, untracked, renames, caps | | `spec/05-context.md` | Finder context, navigation tools, grounding | | `spec/06-llm-protocols.md` | `chat`, `responses`, `anthropic` adapters | | `spec/07-agent-loop.md` | Agent loop, token ledger, wrap-up | | `spec/08-finder-prompts.md` | Finder stage, findings, fingerprints, prompts | | `spec/09-verify-render.md` | Verifier, report rendering, textsafe | | `spec/10-records.md` | Run records, bundles, `runs` / `runs show` | | `spec/11-snapshot.md` | Snapshots, `runs checkout` / `runs prune` | | `spec/12-gate.md` | Gate (pre-commit hook), `ignore` | | `spec/13-login.md` | `login`, `logout`, OpenRouter PKCE | | `spec/14-doctor-init-skill.md` | `doctor`, `init` starter, `skill` | | `spec/15-packaging.md` | Build, tests, lint, CI, release, M0, M6 | ### Modules One module directory per area under the source root, mirroring the Go packages: cli, config, trust, credentials, scope, context, llm (protocol adapters, SSE parser, agent loop, ledger), review (finder), prompts, verify, render, textsafe, record, runbundle, snapshot, gate, skill. Two shared modules sit below them: - **gocompat**: Go-compatibility helpers that parity depends on -- `isGoSpace` / `goTrimSpace` (Go `unicode.IsSpace`, which differs from JS `\\s` and `trim`), `goExt` (Go `filepath.Ext`, which differs from Node `path.extname` for names like `docs/.md`), `truncateUtf8`, and `formatFixed2` (Go `%.2f`, which rounds exact ties to even unlike `toFixed`), plus the Go-style duration parser. - **fsutil**: atomic file writes with explicit modes (trust record, credentials, hook files, run records). The CLI has one entry, bundled to one file. There is no library API. ### Deliberate changes from the Go baseline Product changes (from `spec/00-decisions.md`): 1. No `completion` command. 2. No Windows support (`os: darwin, linux`). 3. Node `>=22.18`, checked at startup with a clear error. 4. Trust hash v2 (ADR-0003); Go-era approvals are not honored. 5. `DEBUG=roast:*` tracing, never including bodies, headers, prompts, or credentials. 6. Version `2.0.0`. Tightenings and fixes (resolved open questions): 7. JSON keys match exactly (case-sensitive) everywhere: `.roast.json`, `credentials.json`, bundle manifests, model tool arguments. JSON `null` still means \"absent\", as in Go. 8. Every HTTP redirect is refused as an endpoint error: inference requests and key validation alike. 9. Provider error bodies are read at most 32 KiB. 10. `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` so the secret-file denylist cannot be disabled from the environment. 11. A run ID must be a plain name: `.`, `..`, and path separators are rejected. 12. `runs checkout` clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, and resolves a relative target directory against the working directory once, used consistently by every git call. 13. Cached tokens are subtracted once, not twice, for `chat` and `responses` usage. 14. A `chat` stream chunk without usage keeps the last reported usage instead of clearing it. 15. Diff name lists and untracked-file lists use `-z`, and diffs run with `core.quotePath=false`; non-ASCII paths are no longer skipped or misattributed. 16. The staged generated-file exclusion lists renames' deleted side too. 17. The user's `diff.orderFile` is overridden so diff order is deterministic. 18. The size-fit breakdown uses a stable sort. 19. A one-line hook file without a trailing newline gets one before Roast's block is appended. 20. `confidence_floor` values are validated to 0..1, matching the schema. 21. Anthropic tool definitions carry the full JSON schema, including `additionalProperties`. 22. A second Ctrl-C force-exits with status 130. Ctrl-C at a raw-mode secret prompt is cancellation (exit 5). 23. Unreachable Go code is not ported: branch-survey scope, the `git_diff` tool and the grounder's range corpus, and the test-only helpers `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, `DescribeEndpoint`. 24. File names that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning. ### Cross-cutting rules - **JSON encoding**: no HTML escaping of `<>&` or U+2028/U+2029. Parity is defined on parsed JSON, not bytes. Tool results sent to models go through one encoder in the llm area that sorts object keys, matching Go's map ordering so prompts stay stable. - **Bytes and text**: git output is read as `Buffer`. Diff bytes stay `Buffer` wherever they are hashed or recorded, so digests match Go. Text is decoded with U+FFFD replacement only where it goes to a model or a report. Lone surrogates from model output become U+FFFD when the llm area decodes it; `textsafe` escapes any that remain as `\\uNNNN`. Config validation rejects lone surrogates so the trust hash is always defined. - **Config numbers**: integer fields reject non-integer lexemes (`1.0`, `1e3`) by checking raw token text through the JSONC parser's visitor. - **Output strings**: em dashes in Go's user- and model-facing strings are kept, written as `\\u2014` escapes in TS source; docs and comments never contain the character. - **CLI parsing**: yargs semantics are accepted where they differ from cobra: decimal integers only, `--flag false` may be read as a value, list flags split on plain commas. A parse failure uses the parsed `--error-format` when available. EPIPE on stdout exits quietly with the computed code. - **Process lifetime**: every tool that spawns a process kills it on abort, and the CLI calls `process.exit` after flushing output. - **Runtime**: the `node:zlib` zstd ExperimentalWarning is suppressed (that warning only). Tar archives support PAX path records when writing and reading. Version, commit, and build date are injected at build time; running from source reports `dev`. - **Network**: no proxy library is bundled. The README documents `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`; `doctor` warns when `HTTPS_PROXY` is set but `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` is not. ### Milestones M0 scaffold; M1 config, trust, credentials; M2 scope and context; M3 `chat` protocol, agent loop, finder, verifier, render, records -- first working `npx roast review`; M4 `responses` and `anthropic`; M5 remaining commands; M6 cutover (delete Go, platform packages, launcher; update AGENTS.md; release `2.0.0`). Details in `spec/15-packaging.md`. ## Testing Decisions - A good test drives external behavior and asserts on exit codes, JSON output, files written, and requests the fake server received. No assertions on exact human-readable text, docs, or CSS. - **Primary seam**: the CLI run in-process (`run(argv, io)` with injectable stdin, stdout, stderr, env, cwd) against a `node:http` fake inference server speaking all three protocols, with a swappable `fetch` so profiles on built-in endpoints reach the fake. Prior art: Go's `executeContext` with `httptest`, `providerServer`, `redirectDefaultTransport`, `settingsRepo`, `loginRepo`. - **Lower seams**, only where the CLI is too coarse: the token ledger, trust hashing (frozen test vectors), `textsafe`, the SSE parser, `gocompat`, and the agent loop driven by a scripted fake protocol adapter. - **Swappable module functions** for terminal detection, browser opening, and the browser-login timeout, as in Go. - **Gate**: a real `git commit` against a shell-stub `roast` at `node_modules/.bin/roast`, as in Go's hook execution tests. - Tests that touch records, credentials, trust, or skills chdir into a temporary Git repository and isolate `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, as the Go tests do. - Go tests are ported area by area; each area file's test plan maps Go test names to behaviors and seams. - A temporary differential harness builds the Go binary at `cd08f10` and runs both CLIs against the same fake server, comparing exit codes, parsed JSON, and run records, with an allowlist for the deliberate changes above. It runs in CI on Ubuntu from M1 to M5 and is deleted at M6. - The two rendered user prompts are checked against Go output by a temporary differential test, deleted at M6. - Opt-in live tests (`ROAST_LIVE_*`) are kept and never run in CI. - The allocation-bound test for huge files becomes an RSS bound. ## Out of Scope - Windows support. - A programmatic library API. - Behavior redesigns beyond the deliberate changes above; they come after `2.0.0`. - Tracking or porting future upstream (`bluesky-social/roast`) changes. - Byte-identical compressed output (`.gz`, `.tar.zst`): decompressed bytes must match, compressor output may differ. - Byte-identical help text with cobra. - Bundling a proxy agent. - Git submodules inside snapshots (documented as unsupported). ## Further Notes - Kept as Go does, on purpose: `null` (not `[]`) for empty `notes`, `matches`, and `lines`; `verified: true` with no findings; the monthly index keeping the oldest sighting (the Go comment is wrong, not the behavior); a global `core.hooksPath` affecting `gate disable`; `init` writing to the working directory; doctor checking only user-level skill installs; CLI hints saying bare `roast`; a malformed trust record exiting 4; author routes discarding `--finder-*` overrides; an all-zero finding from invalid `report_finding` JSON; index timestamps in local time and `meta.json` in UTC; default adaptive thinking for `claude-mythos-preview`. - The headless OpenRouter login (no `callback_url`) needs a manual check against the live service before release. - `jsonc-parser` must run with trailing commas allowed: the `init` starter writes one. - `@substrate-system/debug` and its dependencies must pass the license allowlist in M0. - The uncommitted `package.json` lists `yargs`, `zod`, `jsonc-parser` under `dependencies`; M0 moves them to `devDependencies`. ---- TICKET ---- 16-finder-stage.md # 16: Finder stage **Blocked by:** 11, 14, 15, 39, 40 **Touches:** `src/review/types.ts` (`Severity`, `SEVERITIES`, `severityRank`, `parseSeverity`, `WireFinding`, `Finding`, `Result`, `ASSESSMENT`, `DROP`, `effectiveSeverity`, `maxSeverity`, `toFinding`, `Verdict` type placeholder for ticket 21), `src/review/schema.ts` (`WIRE_FINDING_SCHEMA`, `FINDINGS_RESPONSE_SCHEMA`, `decodeWireFinding`, `decodeFindingsResponse`), `src/review/validate.ts` (`validate`, `assignIdentity`), `src/review/fingerprint.ts` (`collapseWhitespace`, `fingerprint`), `src/review/find.ts` (`FindInput`, `FindingAccumulator`, `submitDescription`, `REPORT_FINDING_DESCRIPTION`, `find`), `test/review/`, `test/index.ts` **Spec:** `spec/08-finder-prompts.md` 2.1-2.10, 2.18, 2.21, 3, 5 (\"Severity and result model\", \"Validate\", \"Fingerprint\", \"Find\"); `spec/07-agent-loop.md` 3.3 (`Tool.recordsResult`, `FinalSpec`); `spec.md` \"Deliberate changes\" 7, \"Cross-cutting rules\" (JSON encoding) **Status:** ready-for-agent ## What to build The finder stage as a library: given rendered prompts, an LLM `Client` (ticket 14), exploration tools, and a grounder (ticket 11), `find` runs either one structured call or an interactive conversation and returns a validated `Result`. Severity and the finding model follow 2.1-2.2: rank order with unknown as INFO, `parseSeverity` ignoring case and surrounding space, `effectiveSeverity` applying only confirmed, strictly lower recalibrations, and `maxSeverity` returning -1 with no findings. `Finding.lines` is `number[]|null` so an empty list serializes as `null`. The `Verdict` type is declared here minimally; the verifier stage that fills it is ticket 21. Tool schemas (2.3) are frozen constants whose JSON matches the baseline exactly, with sorted keys. Decoding final and reported arguments follows Go `json.Unmarshal` semantics (2.4): exact, case-sensitive keys (deliberate change 7), `null` as absent, and the documented coercions and errors. `validate(resp, floor, ground)` applies the drop gates in the 2.8 order with the 2.7 drop reasons, canonicalizes severity, and promotes or demotes the assessment. `assignIdentity` gives every kept and dropped finding a v4 UUID and a short `R-` alias unique as a prefix among all ids (2.9); the id source is injectable so tests can force a shared prefix. `fingerprint` is the 16-hex SHA-256 contract of 2.10, using Go whitespace rules from `gocompat` (U+0085 is whitespace, U+FEFF is not). `find` (2.18): single-shot uses `completeStructured` with tool `findings`; interactive offers the caller's tools, then `report_finding` (`recordsResult: true`, never throws, feeds `FindingAccumulator` and the `capture` sink), then the final `submit_findings`. Cutoffs (no final call, work deadline, token budget, turn budget) produce an incomplete result with `INCOMPLETE_NO_SUBMISSION` or the accumulated findings and the mapped stop reason, never `NO_FINDINGS`; other client errors propagate unchanged. Wire text with U+2014 is written as `\\u2014` in source. Out of scope: rendering (17), records and the finding spill file (18; here `capture` is just a callback), CLI wiring (20), survey-specific CLI completion logic (25). ## Acceptance criteria - [ ] `parseSeverity` accepts any case with surrounding space, rejects `\"\"`; unknown severities rank as INFO. - [ ] `effectiveSeverity` applies only a confirmed, strictly lower recalibration; refuted, insufficient_context, invalid, equal, or higher keep the original. `maxSeverity` uses effective severity and is -1 with no findings. - [ ] `validate` produces each drop reason, respects gate order, treats whitespace-only quote or fix as missing, applies the confidence floor boundary as in Go, and promotes or demotes the assessment. - [ ] Lower-case severities are canonicalized and kept; an unknown severity is dropped as `invalid_severity`. - [ ] After `assignIdentity`, every kept and dropped finding has a v4 UUID and an `R-` alias of at least 6 hex characters that is a unique prefix among all ids, including when a seeded id source forces a shared prefix. - [ ] A finding with no lines serializes `lines` as `null`. - [ ] `fingerprint` is 16 hex characters, stable across whitespace differences, ignores the description, changes with file or code, treats U+0085 as whitespace and U+FEFF as not, and matches a checked-in vector computed by the Go baseline. - [ ] `WIRE_FINDING_SCHEMA` and `FINDINGS_RESPONSE_SCHEMA` deep-equal the baseline JSON in 2.3. - [ ] `decodeFindingsResponse` rejects a wrong-case key such as `\"Severity\"`, treats `null` as absent, and otherwise matches the 2.4 decoding rules. - [ ] With a scripted fake adapter: single-shot returns the validated structured response; interactive returns validated submitted findings. - [ ] A conversation that never submits yields assessment `INCOMPLETE_NO_SUBMISSION`, `incomplete: true`, a note that keeps the cause, and stop reasons `deadline`, `token_budget`, and turn budget mapped per 2.18. - [ ] Findings reported through `report_finding` reach the `capture` sink, survive a cutoff, and make the result incomplete; if all are dropped the assessment stays `INCOMPLETE_NO_SUBMISSION`. - [ ] Submission merges reported findings; the accumulator dedups by fingerprint with last-wins content and first-appearance order. - [ ] A successful wrap-up after the work deadline is incomplete with stop reason `deadline`; a non-cutoff client error propagates unchanged. - [ ] Driving a real `chat` client against the `node:http` fake server (ticket 13), the wrap-up turn keeps reported findings for report-then-submit, report-and-submit together, report-only, and report alongside refused exploration, with the expected incompleteness and request counts. - [ ] `report_finding` with invalid JSON arguments records an empty finding that `validate` drops as `invalid_severity`. - [ ] `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and the TS test suite pass, and the Go build and Go tests still pass. These are records of earlier runs. The ticket or spec may have c